The trolley problem reveals that most people's intuitions are not consistently consequentialist — we apply different moral rules to acts versus omissions, even when outcomes are identical, because the means carry independent moral weight.
International humanitarian law (the laws of war) explicitly prohibits certain means — torture, targeting civilians — even when commanders argue military necessity justifies them, because the prohibition is more valuable than any particular tactical advantage.
Machiavelli himself acknowledged that a prince who relies on fraud and force undermines the stability he seeks — means constrain the ends that are ultimately achievable.
